<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Test project for shared/PowerFramework.Contracts, the published cross-service boundary:
    Proto/common.v1.proto, Proto/dataservices.v1.proto, Proto/persistence.v1.proto,
    OpenApi/security.v1.yaml and OpenApi/gateway.v1.yaml.

    HAND-AUTHORED ON PURPOSE. `dotnet new xunit3` does not exist in the pinned SDK and
    `dotnet new xunit` scaffolds the xunit v2 line with an older test SDK and coverage package,
    so templating this project would silently downgrade the test stack.

    Everything else is inherited from the repository-root Directory.Build.props by directory
    walk-up: net10.0, C# latest, nullable enabled, implicit usings enabled, warnings as errors,
    .NET analysers, and central package management. Those are mandatory quality gates, so they
    are deliberately NOT restated here and must never be relaxed per project. Adding NoWarn, or
    turning off warnings as errors, or disabling nullable to make a test compile would be a
    violation rather than a fix: the fix belongs in the offending test file. Package versions
    live in the repository-root Directory.Packages.props, which is why every PackageReference
    below is versionless, without exception: Directory.Build.props disables central version
    overriding outright, so a VersionOverride here would fail restore with NU1013.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- MANDATORY for xunit.v3, which runs tests from a self hosting executable. Without Exe the
         project still compiles but no test is discovered, and the coverage gate then reads a
         Cobertura report that contains no data. This is the single property that distinguishes a
         v3 test project from a v2 one. -->
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="Test stack and OpenAPI object model : all versionless, versions from Directory.Packages.props">
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.v3" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" />
    <!-- Produces coverage.cobertura.xml, the exact artifact the 80 percent line coverage gate is
         measured from, when the test command collects XPlat Code Coverage. -->
    <PackageReference Include="coverlet.collector" />

    <!-- Centrally pinned at 2.11.0, and that pin is mandatory: 2.0.0 carries NuGet advisory
         NU1903, while the 3.x line breaks the build because the 10.0.x OpenAPI source generator
         is compiled against the 2.x object model and emits CS0200. This project is where that
         pin gets its regression guard, which is why it takes the reference directly. -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.OpenApi" />

    <!--
      THE PACKAGE SET ABOVE PLUS THIS ONE IS THE WHOLE PACKAGE SET. SIX ENTRIES, NO SEVENTH.

      Every reference here is versionless and resolves against the repository-root
      Directory.Packages.props, whose sixteen PackageVersion rows are the complete approved
      dependency inventory for this refactor.

      WHY THIS PACKAGE IS HERE, AND WHY ITS VERSION IS NOT WRITTEN IN THIS FILE.

      Microsoft.OpenApi 2.11.0 ships JSON and YAML *writers* plus a JSON *reader* only. Its
      assembly exposes OpenApiJsonReader, OpenApiJsonWriter and OpenApiYamlWriter, and no YAML
      reader of any kind (verified by inspecting the published package). Both contract documents
      are YAML, so loading and validating them against the pinned object model is physically
      impossible without this reader. This project is its only consumer, because it is the only
      project that READS a contract document rather than serving one.

      Microsoft.OpenApi.YamlReader publishes an exactly version locked 2.11.0 whose dependencies
      are Microsoft.OpenApi 2.11.0, SharpYaml 2.1.4 and System.Text.Json 8.0.5, all MIT licensed.
      Being locked to 2.11.0, it cannot drag the mandatory pin off that version. System.Text.Json
      resolves to the net10.0 shared framework rather than to a package, so SharpYaml 2.1.4 is the
      only net addition to the resolved graph. Neither carries a NuGet advisory at these versions,
      so restore stays audit clean, which matters because warnings are errors.

      THE VERSION LIVES CENTRALLY, LIKE EVERY OTHER ONE, and this reference is versionless in
      consequence. An earlier form of this file supplied the version here with VersionOverride, on
      the reasoning that doing so let the project restore from a clean checkout without editing a
      file outside its own folder. That reasoning was wrong on both halves. Directory.Packages.props
      is part of the same clean checkout, so amending it is not an external edit; and the override
      restored cleanly precisely because it sits in the gap between central package management's two
      guards - NU1008 catches a bare Version attribute and NU1010 catches a package with no central
      entry, while an override is neither - so it quietly re established a per project version
      authority for one package. That is the drift the central manifest exists to prevent.
      Directory.Build.props now sets CentralPackageVersionOverrideEnabled to false, so a
      VersionOverride anywhere in the tree is a hard restore error (NU1013) rather than a silent
      divergence.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.OpenApi.YamlReader" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- The subject under test, and the only supplier of the generated Protobuf message types and
         of the gRPC service base classes and client stubs (generated with GrpcServices Both).
         Google.Protobuf arrives transitively through it, so it is deliberately not referenced
         explicitly here: an explicit reference would risk a version conflict. -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\PowerFramework.Contracts\PowerFramework.Contracts.csproj" />

    <!--
      NOT A STRAY REFERENCE, AND NOT TO BE TIDIED AWAY. PowerFramework.Contracts declares zero
      project references by design, because the contracts project is the boundary definition and
      carries no behaviour of its own. The consequence is that the numeric agreement between the
      RetCode enum in Proto/common.v1.proto and the ported constants in
      PowerFramework.Shared.Kernel/RetCode.cs has no compile-time edge anywhere else in the tree.

      Kernel is referenced here so the fidelity tests can read their expectations FROM the Kernel
      constants rather than restating literals, which makes this project the single place where
      that otherwise review-time-only invariant is enforced mechanically. It is also why this
      project is sequenced behind Kernel.

      Scope is deliberately limited to these two: no service project and no other shared library
      is referenced, because a shared test project reaching into a service would invert the
      layering that keeps the published contracts the only cross-service coupling.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\PowerFramework.Shared.Kernel\PowerFramework.Shared.Kernel.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>


  <!--
    REPOSITORY ROOT, EMBEDDED SO THIS PROJECT'S ON DISK LOCATORS DO NOT DEPEND ON WHERE ITS OUTPUT WENT
    This project reads real files from the source tree, and it finds them by walking up from
    AppContext.BaseDirectory for a marker. That walk holds only while the output sits inside the
    checkout, so a test run whose artifacts path option names a directory outside the tree - a supported
    and entirely valid way to run the suite - left every one of those locators with no ancestor
    carrying the marker, and they threw for a reason unrelated to the code under test.

    The value comes from the PowerFrameworkRepositoryRoot property in the repository root
    Directory.Build.props, which resolves it from that file's own location. It is turned into assembly
    metadata HERE, in each test project, rather than there: a service assembly ships inside a container
    image and must carry no build machine path, and the root property sheet keeps its documented rule of
    introducing no project level item. The locators still verify their marker and still keep the walk as
    the fallback, so an absent or stale value degrades to the previous behaviour rather than misreading
    a different tree.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <AssemblyMetadata Include="PowerFrameworkRepositoryRoot" Value="$(PowerFrameworkRepositoryRoot)" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
